  "query_preview": "..An upper low is centered over northwest Arizona\nearly this morning, and is forecast to move to near 4 corners by\nmid day and off into Colorado by afternoon. Instability and lift\nwith the low will produce scattered showers and a few\nthunderstorms, mainly this morning, primarily north of I-40 but\nfocused in northeast Arizona. With colder air moving in, snow\nlevels will be around 7000 feet, with some light accumulation\nabove 7500-8000 feet from the Kaibab Plateau to the Chuska \nMountains. The ADOT cam at Jacob Lake (7900 ft) shows a dusting \nof snow as of 130 AM. By this afternoon, showers and isolated\nthunderstorms will be focused in far northeast Arizona, ending by\nsunset. Elsewhere around the region, look for gradually clearing\nskies today with northwest breezes 10-15 mph and highs 10-15 \ndegrees below normal. The early mornings will also be chilly \nacross the region the next couple days, and a Freeze Warning \ncontinues through 8 AM this morning for the western half of \nCoconino County including Flagstaff, Williams, and Grand Canyon \nNorth and South Rims. \n\nThursday through Friday - a weak upper low is forecast to cut off\nfrom SW Arizona to northern Baja, with strong surface high pressure\ndiving southward to our east through the Rockies and plains states.\nThis will produce tight east/northeast pressure gradients in our \nregion, leading to gusty winds. Sustained speeds of 20-30 mph with\ngusts 35-45 mph are currently forecast for much of the region, peaking\nThursday evening into Friday. The strongest winds in this pattern\nare typically along and just south of the Mogollon Rim and Yavapai\nCounty mountains. Some moisture could also move back into the region\nfrom the southeast by Friday night into the weekend, and a low (10-20%)\nchance of showers and thunderstorms is included in our latest forecasts.\nRain chances may increase as we get closer, depending on the track\nof the weak upper low as it ejects northeastward over the weekend\nand possible interaction with deeper moisture associated with\nTropical Cyclone Simon. The official NHC forecast for Simon\nweakens it to a depression in the central Gulf of California by\nSunday. While most of the ensemble guidance keeps the deeper \nmoisture to our east, a small percentage bring it farther west, so\nthis will be something to monitor. Daytime highs look to stay \nbelow normal into the weekend. Next week is looking unsettled as\nanother low pressure system is forecast to set up off the California\ncoast and eventually move into our area with additional rain chances.",
